 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Complex Subtraction Imaging
-An imaging technique used to enhance the visualization of changes in tissue contrast by subtracting a reference image from a current image [S2]. Unlike the volume-based residual DVR, this is often applied to image streams (e.g., MRI) [S2]. The reference image can be fixed in time or dynamic (to approximate a time derivative) [S2]. It is used in interventional guidance to highlight temporal changes in tissue properties [S2].
+An imaging technique used to enhance the visualization of changes in tissue contrast by subtracting a reference image from a current image [S2]. It sets itself apart by operating on 2D image streams rather than 3D volumes [S2]. The reference image can be fixed in time or dynamic (to approximate a time derivative) [S2]. It is chosen when temporal changes in tissue contrast need to be highlighted in interventional guidance [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of voxels $N$ for the residual computation step ($O(N)$), assuming constant-time transfer function evaluation. The final rendering cost depends on the chosen DVR algorithm applied to the residual field.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for two input volumes, two transfer functions, and one output residual volume ($O(N)$).
-*   **Strengths:** Directly highlights where optimization or changes in data structure affect the visual output; handles transparency correctly via pre-multiplication [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Only reveals differences in the *rendered* appearance, not raw data differences; sensitive to transfer function choices [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When comparing two volume datasets or two transfer functions to identify regions of significant visual discrepancy [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When the goal is to visualize absolute values or when transfer function differences are not of interest [S1, unofficial].
